## Running GPT-OSS-120B Locally

To run GPT-OSS-120B, you’ll need a high-end GPU setup (the official docs recommend an 80 GB NVIDIA GPU). You can deploy it via frameworks like Hugging Face Transformers, vLLM, or tools like Ollama. For example, using Transformers: first install the libraries, then load the model ID `"openai/gpt-oss-120b"` on GPU. A Python snippet might look like:

```python
from transformers import pipeline
import torch

# Load GPT-OSS-120B with automatic device placement
pipe = pipeline(
    "text-generation",
    model="openai/gpt-oss-120b",
    torch_dtype="auto",
    device_map="auto",
)
# Example prompt
prompt = {"role": "user", "content": "Explain quantum mechanics clearly and concisely."}
output = pipe([prompt], max_new_tokens=128)
print(output[0]["generated_text"])
```

This code (adapted from the official model card) will run GPT-OSS-120B on any available GPU.  Because the model uses MXFP4 quantization, it can fit on one H100 (80 GB) GPU. A smaller variant, GPT-OSS-20B, can even run within a standard 16 GB GPU or a laptop as Simon Willison demonstrated. In practice, community users report the 20B model running on a 32 GB Mac with only \~12 GB RAM. (For large-scale use, you can also split GPT-OSS-120B across multiple GPUs or use vLLM for optimized serving.)

## Potential Use Cases

GPT-OSS-120B’s combination of power and openness enables many applications:

* **Local Chatbots/Assistants:** Deploy a Discord bot or customer support agent entirely on your servers. Without API limits or costs, the bot can handle unlimited chats with the style and memory you define. The open model lets you fine-tune it on your community’s language (Hyperfocus jargon, DOOK mode, etc.).
* **Multi-Agent Systems:** Use it as the “brain” for an agentic environment. GPT-OSS can interact with tools (web search, calculators, Python) in real time, so it can coordinate multiple tasks (e.g. a “revenue autopilot” that generates and tests marketing copy on the fly).
* **Emotional & Personalized AI:** Build an AI companion or character with tailored personality. Fine-tune on sentiment or persona data to create an emotionally aware chatbot (the user’s “EmotiSphere model”), all running locally for privacy.
* **Content Generation & Documentation:** Automate copywriting, blogging, or coding assistance. For example, have GPT-OSS-120B draft product descriptions or generate code snippets on demand. Its strong reasoning means it can also summarize and document legacy systems (the “ChaosGenius auto-doc” use case) without any rate limits.
* **On-Device Inference:** With quantization and MoE efficiency, you could run scaled-down GPT-OSS models on-premises. Even GPT-OSS-20B runs on edge hardware, enabling offline AI features in apps or games (like an “Agent Playground” or mobile AI features).

Each of these benefits leverages the open nature of the model: **no external API or throttling, full data control, and the ability to fine-tune or extend the model** for your exact needs.

## Performance and Limitations

GPT-OSS-120B is very capable but not unlimited. It *nearly* matches GPT-4-class performance in reasoning, but GPT-4 (and larger proprietary models) can still outmatch it on the hardest tasks. OpenAI’s benchmarks show GPT-OSS-120B “outperforms o3-mini and matches or exceeds o4-mini” on coding, math, and health questions. In practice, outside tests found it slightly below GPT-4o on expert quizzes. For many applications, however, GPT-OSS-120B’s power is “plenty” – and you can further boost it via fine-tuning.

In terms of requirements, you **will need beefy hardware** (at least one 80 GB GPU) for full performance. (However, quantized/LoRA versions may run on multi-GPU or 24+ GB setups.) Despite the model’s safeguards, any powerful LLM can produce errors or undesired content, so apply standard alignment checks. OpenAI reports they evaluated GPT-OSS-120B with adversarial safety tuning and found it “comparable to \[their] frontier models on internal safety benchmarks”, but responsible use and monitoring are still advised.

## Example: Python Code Prompt

Because GPT-OSS-120B supports function calling, you can even have it generate and execute code. For instance, using the OpenAI API style chat format (compatible with GPT-OSS), you might send:

```
User: "Write a Python function to compute Fibonacci numbers recursively."
```

The model can return a structured function call with code. In a local pipeline, you would capture that output and execute it. (This is similar to GPT-4’s code features, now available offline in GPT-OSS.)

```python
from transformers import pipeline

chat = pipeline("text-generation", model="openai/gpt-oss-120b", device_map="auto", torch_dtype="auto")
messages = [
    {"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant. Provide code in markdown."},
    {"role": "user", "content": "Write a Python function to compute Fibonacci numbers recursively."}
]
result = chat(messages, max_new_tokens=100)
print(result[0]["generated_text"])
```

This will generate and print a Python function. The “assistant” message in `result[0]["generated_text"]` would contain code (enclosed in markdown) that you could then run or adapt. GPT-OSS-120B even supports executing such code in-line if set up properly.

